<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tonle Sap</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tonle Sap is a large freshwater lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> connected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong River</a>, which swells greatly during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> season. Its seasonal flooding and rich fisheries supported dense populations and intensive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> agriculture. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a> treat the Tonle Sap region as part of the environmental foundation of Khmer power around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tonle-sap.jpg" alt="Tonle Sap" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Deror_avi · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cambodia,_Tonle_Sap_IMG_3285.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tonle Sap, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> name means Fresh River or, commonly, Great Lake, lies in the northwest of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> plain in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>. It is the largest freshwater lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. Its basin covers about 86,000 square kilometres. The Tonle Sap River, about 120 kilometres long, joins the lake to the Mekong.</p><p>The lake changes enormously with the seasons. In the dry season it covers about 2,500 to 3,000 square kilometres and is about a metre deep. In the rainy season, from May to October, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a>'s rising water reverses the flow of the Tonle Sap River, so that it runs into the lake for about six months and out again for the other six. The lake then reaches up to 16,000 square kilometres and a depth of 9 to 14 metres, and acts as a natural flood reservoir for the Mekong system.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The lake has long been a vital area for fishing and farming in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, and it largely sustained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>, described as the largest pre-industrial settlement complex in history. Fish came into the flooded forests to spawn, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> floods brought large numbers of fry. Historical research indicates that the people of Angkor dug large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> to hold water in the wet season and released it in the dry season through an irrigation system, which doubled and sometimes tripled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> crops each year. That network no longer exists, and farmers now take one crop a year.</p><p>Henri Mouhot described the lake in 1864 as covering an immense space for more than five months of the year, swollen by the current of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a>. Its most productive fishing grounds were leased by the government for more than a century. Many people of Vietnamese origin live in floating villages on the lake, as do <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> communities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>About 1.2 million people live around the lake and fish its waters, and the catch supplies much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>'s freshwater fish. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> designated the area a Biosphere Reserve in 1997, and a royal decree of 2001 made the lake and nine surrounding provinces the Tonle Sap Biosphere Reserve, with core, buffer and transition zones. The lake supports at least 149 fish species, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> giant catfish.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tonle Sap” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The empire's rise was made possible by the fertile floodplains of the <b>Tonle Sap</b> and Mekong systems, which supported intensive wet-rice agriculture, and by its position within regional trade networks linking India and China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Ayutthaya's sack of Angkor around 1431, the Khmer court under Ponhea Yat moved south toward the Mekong and the <b>Tonle Sap</b> confluence, eventually settling at Phnom Penh.</blockquote><a href="/state/post-angkorian-cambodia">Post-Angkorian Cambodia</a></li><li><blockquote>It covered the lower Mekong, the <b>Tonle Sap</b> region and the delta, the old lands of Funan.</blockquote><a href="/state/water-chenla">Water Chenla</a></li></ul></section>
